- qjs-swallows-uncaught-exceptions-with-native-module: NOT a qjs-opencv
  bug - root cause lives in the `qjs` CLI / QuickJS engine itself (a
  separate project, not this repo), recorded here only because it was
  discovered while chasing an unrelated-looking "process exits silently"
  symptom. Confirmed the opencv module is not implicated:
  `import * as std from 'std'; gc();` (no opencv involved at all)
  reproduces the same silent exit(0) with no error printed, whereas the
  identical script with no import correctly prints the exception and
  exits 1. So this is a bug in how `qjs` reports uncaught top-level
  exceptions once *any* native module has been dynamically loaded via the
  module system - out of scope to fix from qjs-opencv. Worth reporting
  upstream / fixing in the quickjs project directly if it matters (it
  currently means any qjs-opencv script's uncaught errors are invisible).

    import * as std from 'std';
    console.log("before");
    gc(); // or any other uncaught exception
    console.log("after"); // never reached, and no error is printed either;
                           // process exits 0. Without the import, the
                           // same script prints the ReferenceError and
                           // exits 1 as expected.

- cmakelists-option-declared-after-use: `option(USE_FEATURE2D ...)` and
  `option(USE_BARCODE ...)` in `CMakeLists.txt` are declared at line ~315-316,
  but both variables are already read by `if(USE_FEATURE2D)` (line ~136) and
  `if(USE_BARCODE)` (line ~158) earlier in the same file. On a truly fresh
  build directory (no prior `CMakeCache.txt`), CMake treats the not-yet-declared
  variable as undefined/false at that point, so both `if()` blocks are
  silently skipped: `-DUSE_FEATURE2D=1`/`-DUSE_BARCODE=1` never get added,
  `HAVE_OPENCV2_XFEATURES2D_HPP`/`HAVE_OPENCV2_BARCODE_HPP` never get probed,
  and `FEATURE2D_SOURCE`/`BARCODE_SOURCE` never get set - `js_feature2d.cpp`
  and `js_barcode_detector.cpp` end up compiled without the flags their code
  depends on (found while adding new capability checks inside the
  `if(USE_FEATURE2D)` block for the OpenCV 5 migration and discovering they
  silently never ran on a clean configure; existing build directories masked
  this because their `CMakeCache.txt` already had `USE_FEATURE2D`/`USE_BARCODE`
  cached ON from before this ordering regression). Fixed as part of the
  OpenCV 5 migration work by moving both `option()` calls above their first
  use.

    rm -rf build/some-fresh-dir
    cmake -B build/some-fresh-dir .   # no -DUSE_FEATURE2D=... on the command line
    grep USE_FEATURE2D build/some-fresh-dir/compile_commands.json  # absent

- cmakelists-broken-barcode-check-library-exists: `check_library_exists(opencv_objdetect
  _ZN2cv7barcode15BarcodeDetectorC1Ev "${OPENCV_LIBDIR}" OPENCV_BARCODE)` inside the
  `if(USE_BARCODE)` block of `CMakeLists.txt` (originally line ~151, right after
  `set(BARCODE_SOURCE js_barcode_detector.cpp)`) fails configuration outright on a fresh build
  directory with `CMake Error ... No known features for CXX compiler "" version .` from inside
  `CheckLibraryExists.cmake`'s internal `try_compile`, aborting the whole cmake run. This was
  masked for a long time by [[cmakelists-option-declared-after-use]]: with that bug in place,
  `USE_BARCODE` was always false at this point in the file, so this block - and this broken
  check - never actually executed on a from-scratch configure; only after fixing the option()
  ordering (as part of the OpenCV 5 migration work) did this second latent bug surface. The
  check's result variable, `OPENCV_BARCODE`, is never read anywhere else in the CMake project
  (confirmed by grep) - it was already dead code even when it worked, if it ever did. Removed the
  call entirely as part of the OpenCV 5 migration work rather than trying to fix its arguments,
  since nothing depends on its result.

    rm -rf build/some-fresh-dir
    cmake -B build/some-fresh-dir -DUSE_BARCODE=ON .
    # CMake Error ... No known features for CXX compiler "" version .
    # -- Configuring incomplete, errors occurred!
